  "name": "Edison International",
  "plain_analysis": {
    "positives": [
      "If this company sold everything it owns and paid off everything it owes, there would be real money left over. It owns more than it owes.",
      "The profit behind each share of stock has been growing at a healthy pace over the last few years.",
      "Using simple math based on its profit and what it owns, the stock looks priced well below what the business is roughly worth - a real discount.",
      "This business makes more profit on the money it uses than that money actually costs to raise, whether borrowed or put in by investors. That's a real edge over competitors - something people call a 'moat.'"
    ],
    "worries": [
      "It has borrowed a lot of money - what it owes is more than twice what the business itself is worth, which makes it riskier if things go wrong.",
      "It might not have enough cash or easy-to-sell things on hand to cover the bills it owes soon.",
      "After paying for everything it needs to run and grow the business, it's actually spending more cash than it's bringing in."
    ]
  },
